# zmk-module-bmp-extender-lcd

config LS0XX_SWVCOM
    bool "Sharp LS0xx memory display driver with software VCOM toggling"
    default y
    depends on DT_HAS_SHARP_LS0XX_SWVCOM_ENABLED
    depends on DISPLAY
    help
      Sharp MIP display driver based on Zephyr's ls0xx, adding periodic
      software VCOM inversion via the serial command V bit for wirings
      without an EXTCOMIN line.

config LS0XX_SWVCOM_HEAL_REFRESH_MS
    int "Self-healing periodic full-panel refresh interval (ms) for the rotated MIP driver, 0 to disable"
    default 2000
    depends on LS0XX_SWVCOM
    help
      Real-hardware feedback (2026-08-05, tecla-cero) showed random
      speckle/streak corruption accumulating in the panel's own bistable
      pixel memory while the BLE host was out of range -- suspected
      external causes are voltage sag on an out-of-spec supply rail
      during advertising radio current spikes, and/or 2.4GHz EMI coupling
      into jumper wiring (see README.md, "制約・注意点"). The row-level
      dirty-diff sender in the
      driver only re-sends rows whose *intended* content changed, so it
      cannot detect or fix corruption that happens outside its control;
      before the diff sender existed, frequent full-panel redraws
      happened to paper over the corruption on every unrelated redraw,
      which is what read as "flicker". This makes the driver blind-resend
      its shadow framebuffer (known-good content) to the whole panel on a
      periodic timer, self-healing any corruption within one interval
      regardless of whether LVGL thinks anything changed. Firmware cannot
      fix the underlying electrical cause; this only bounds how long
      corruption can persist. Only effective when the driver's rotation
      is enabled (the shadow framebuffer this reads from only exists on
      that path); the driver guards the whole feature with
      `#if LS0XX_ROTATION != 0`, so in rotation=0 builds this symbol
      compiles to nothing regardless of its value. Set to 0 to disable.
      The extra ~12ms SPI transfer every interval is negligible next to
      typical values (the display has no continuous animation); the
      timer's own device is unaffected by ZMK_SLEEP/System OFF since
      ext-power is cut first.

config LS0XX_SWVCOM_INIT_FILL_BLACK
    bool "Send an all-black fill instead of CLEAR on driver init"
    default n
    depends on LS0XX_SWVCOM
    help
      At driver init, send an all-rows black (ink) fill instead of the
      usual white CLEAR command. Useful when the application's first
      frame is black (e.g. a boot splash), so the retained
      previous-session image (Sharp MIP panels keep the last written
      image on their bistable pixel memory across power cycles) cuts
      straight to black with no white blink in between. Only effective
      when the driver's rotation is enabled (the shadow-framebuffer path);
      writing all rows pins every pixel to a known state exactly like
      CLEAR would, so this is a drop-in replacement for that
      initialization step.
